descending to the deep the ocean  twilight zone where daylight barely  penetrates yet life still thrives Sky  News joined marine scientists from the  nekton mission in Bermuda three years  ago now we're rejoining the team for a  new voyage to explore Aldabra a remote  coral atoll in the Seychelles and  broadcasting the first ever live TV news  bulletins from under the water to draw  attention to the plight of the world's  oceans plastic pollution climate change  and overfishing threaten coral reefs the  most diverse ecosystem on the planet  this mission will push the boundaries of  both science and broadcasting the  satellite dishes are now here but the  mothership still needs to be cabled up  and rigged with cameras and still to  come the two mini submarines that will  take us and you down into the deep for  three live one-hour programs the scale  of this is quite extraordinary the  oceans affair is being refitted in  Bremerhaven on Germany's North Sea coast  once in the Seychelles researchers will  dive up to 300 metres below the surface  surveying deep water reefs that no one  has been to before they expect to find  hundreds if not thousands of new species  I think we've been looking up when we  should have been looking down I mean  we've had this great era of space  exploration and that has pushed back the  frontiers of our knowledge but the deep  ocean is the last great frontier it's  the last major piece of our planet we  still don't know about coral reefs  Harbor a quarter of all marine species  but climate change is warming the Seas  and on current trends 99% of shallow  water coral will die the Indian Ocean is  one of the least explored areas in the  world  the nekton scientist fear we could be  the last generation to see the reefs in  all their glory this change is happening  very quickly so the last 50 years coral  reefs have experienced changes that they  have an experience for the last  thousands of years so we are really kind  of in a rush to try and understand the  the have kind of baseline information of  coral reefs so we understand how they  will change in the future  for two years sky ocean rescue has  highlighted the scourge of plastic by  taking viewers down into the deep we  hope to inspire global action to save  the coral on which so much marine life  depends Tamas more Sky News Bremerhaven  in Germany
 

